WEBVTT

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:02.380
- There's a bunch of words
for model space.

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:05.060
There's local space,
there's object space.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:06.887
So at one point,
Scott and I

00:00:06.887 --> 00:00:08.563
were arguing about this,
 and we're like,

00:00:08.563 --> 00:00:11.746
"We got it. Paul Neale space."
- Exactly.

00:00:11.746 --> 00:00:14.942
You make the 3D models,
and then we do things with them.

00:00:14.942 --> 00:00:16.460
- Yeah.
So there you go.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.180
- So it's such an honor
to have you here.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.240
You're famous in our program,

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.169
because when we build matrices

00:00:22.169 --> 00:00:25.549
that get you between the spaces,
we call them Paul Neale space.

00:00:25.549 --> 00:00:27.359
And I've had some students
think that you're this

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:28.960
fictional character.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.914
I'm like, "No,
Paul Neale exists."

00:00:30.914 --> 00:00:33.889
So when you come into
the classroom, they go wild.

00:00:33.889 --> 00:00:36.495
- Man, something's going to
happen when I retire.

00:00:36.495 --> 00:00:38.104
Then I'll become fictional,
I guess.

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:40.871
- I'll have to come visit you.
- Yeah.

00:00:40.871 --> 00:00:43.891
- So, for this video,
I asked you to make a teapot.

00:00:43.891 --> 00:00:45.730
Like, we'll get
to that later.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:47.267
We'll see how you made it.

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:50.280
But Cassidy's friend
in industrial design, thank you,

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.307
built a 3D...

00:00:52.307 --> 00:00:54.511
a 3D-printed teapot.

00:00:54.511 --> 00:00:56.226
So before we start talking
 about this,

00:00:56.226 --> 00:00:57.880
what's the big deal
with teapots?

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.248
I mean, it seems
to get you both smiling

00:01:00.248 --> 00:01:01.757
when you see a teapot.

00:01:01.757 --> 00:01:03.634
What happening?

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:05.806
- In the beginning years

00:01:05.806 --> 00:01:07.971
of computer graphics,

00:01:07.971 --> 00:01:10.679
the university in Utah came up

00:01:10.679 --> 00:01:13.558
with this little design,
and it was

00:01:13.558 --> 00:01:16.946
originally made
 of NURBS and things

00:01:16.946 --> 00:01:18.938
to get the curves and all this.

00:01:18.938 --> 00:01:21.185
And it just became
sort of the symbol

00:01:21.185 --> 00:01:23.277
of graphic testing,

00:01:23.277 --> 00:01:25.320
and it has existed forever.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.078
We now refer to it as

00:01:27.078 --> 00:01:29.142
the Utah teapot.

00:01:29.142 --> 00:01:30.850
- Yeah, it's got compound
curves, right?

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.478
Which were difficult
to figure out with Blinn shaders

00:01:33.478 --> 00:01:36.097
to get them, the highlights
to come up and everything.

00:01:36.097 --> 00:01:38.340
So it was kind of
the test bed for years

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.338
of people trying to write
rendering algorithms, right?

00:01:41.338 --> 00:01:43.224
- I think the new test standard

00:01:43.224 --> 00:01:45.793
is called the Stanford bunny.

00:01:45.793 --> 00:01:48.755
It's a scanned,
it's a scanned chocolate bunny.

00:01:48.755 --> 00:01:50.500
- Oh, wow.
- Yeah.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.036
- Is that to get some of
that soft body?

00:01:52.036 --> 00:01:53.730
Like, could it melt or...

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.247
No, no, it was...

00:01:55.247 --> 00:01:57.771
It just had a lot,
a greater resolution.

00:01:57.771 --> 00:02:00.226
This doesn't have a lot
of vertices to it.

00:02:00.226 --> 00:02:03.963
So this is not a very good
model for speed testing.

00:02:03.963 --> 00:02:06.919
It's just too easy
to render nowadays.

00:02:06.919 --> 00:02:08.539
- Nowadays, it's very easy.

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:10.819
We make millions of them
bounce down roads

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:13.380
in simulations and stuff
for the fun of it.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.296
Yeah, just, you know, yeah.

00:02:15.296 --> 00:02:16.860
- And this is a default shape?

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.060
Like, you, you can have spheres,
boxes, and teapots.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.372
- Well, in, in 3ds Max, yes.

00:02:22.372 --> 00:02:23.980
And it...

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.192
People often, in the industry,
 when they started,

00:02:26.192 --> 00:02:29.380
think that it is the 3ds Max
teapot, and it's not.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:32.540
It's the Utah teapot,
as we've discovered.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:35.782
But when they were first
writing 3ds Max,

00:02:35.782 --> 00:02:38.680
one of the programmers, because
now everything's drag and drop.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.297
It used to be when they built
this thing, they would've

00:02:40.297 --> 00:02:42.589
built it point by point.

00:02:42.589 --> 00:02:44.880
Well, now it's just
procedural objects.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.930
We click, drag,
and we have a teapot or a sphere

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.020
or a box or whatever.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.955
So one of the programmers,
as a lark, wrote the teapot

00:02:51.955 --> 00:02:53.580
into a procedural object.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:55.600
You can just click and drag out
and change the resolution

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.720
of it and whatever else.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.240
And it wasn't

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.885
supposed to stay
in the software,

00:03:01.885 --> 00:03:03.398
as far as I understand,
and they...

00:03:03.398 --> 00:03:05.427
You know, somebody said, "No,
you've just got to leave it."

00:03:05.427 --> 00:03:07.772
It's just, you know,
 it pays homage

00:03:07.772 --> 00:03:09.860
to our forefathers kind
of thing, right?

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.389
And so it stayed.

00:03:11.389 --> 00:03:13.134
And, you know, everybody asks,
 "Why is there a teapot?"

00:03:13.134 --> 00:03:16.010
Just for that reason, no other.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:17.956
- Yeah, because when I asked
you, "Could you make us

00:03:17.956 --> 00:03:20.140
a teapot?" the first thing
you said is like, "Why?

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.043
There's a default teapot."

00:03:22.043 --> 00:03:24.678
But I did want to ask you,
like, before we get

00:03:24.678 --> 00:03:27.116
to your model, like,
what was the first thing

00:03:27.116 --> 00:03:28.881
that came to your mind
when you're like,

00:03:28.881 --> 00:03:31.805
"Okay, Umer's asked me
to make a teapot in 3D"?

00:03:31.805 --> 00:03:33.460
- First thing
that comes to mind?

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.604
Well, the first thing was, "Why?
I can click and drag."

00:03:36.604 --> 00:03:38.791
I guess the second is, you know,

00:03:38.791 --> 00:03:41.014
if you're thinking
 about how would I

00:03:41.014 --> 00:03:44.278
break this down to even start
thinking about modeling it,

00:03:44.278 --> 00:03:47.581
what we do is
we break things into

00:03:47.581 --> 00:03:50.360
sort of easier elements,
you know.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:52.526
The lid, for instance,
is a pretty

00:03:52.526 --> 00:03:54.800
easy shape to make.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.266
Just the body of it is a very
easy shape to make

00:03:57.266 --> 00:03:58.954
because it's literally
made the same way

00:03:58.954 --> 00:04:01.441
that we make the lid.

00:04:01.441 --> 00:04:03.227
And then it's trying
to figure out, well, how do you

00:04:03.227 --> 00:04:05.427
extrude out handles
and a spout

00:04:05.427 --> 00:04:07.093
and whatnot,

00:04:07.093 --> 00:04:08.863
and whether or not the lid's

00:04:08.863 --> 00:04:10.875
ever going to come off of it.

00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:13.516
So, you know,
I could've made it so the lid

00:04:13.516 --> 00:04:15.540
was the same shape and it...

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.480
You know, if the lid's never
gonna come off, we don't see

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.481
that in our production
 that we're doing,

00:04:19.481 --> 00:04:21.390
there's no point in having it
as a separate piece.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:24.380
- Yeah, and you
mentioned extruding.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:27.560
That makes me think, like,
you're pulling it apart?

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.227
- Well, you're eventually
down to polygons,

00:04:29.227 --> 00:04:31.308
so that would be
probably better left to

00:04:31.308 --> 00:04:33.460
discussing how I made it,
if you want.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.092
But yeah, so you can take
 polygons and extrude them

00:04:36.092 --> 00:04:38.508
out into more polygons,
essentially.

00:04:38.508 --> 00:04:40.316
So I did that
for things like the handle

00:04:40.316 --> 00:04:42.007
and the spout and whatnot.

00:04:42.007 --> 00:04:44.805
- And you mentioned there
 was a procedural process

00:04:44.805 --> 00:04:47.260
you did as well to...

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:49.447
You said to make this lid,
you could use

00:04:49.447 --> 00:04:51.340
some kind of...

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:52.882
- You know, it's not one
of those things.

00:04:52.882 --> 00:04:55.718
We don't model in just
 pulling points anymore

00:04:55.718 --> 00:04:57.540
like the original Utah teapot.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:59.150
Somebody would've probably
sat there programming

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:02.795
in the numbers of every
point on the surface.

00:05:02.795 --> 00:05:05.877
You know, I started
with a spline, a line,

00:05:05.877 --> 00:05:08.738
a two-dimensional line
 that followed the contour of,

00:05:08.738 --> 00:05:10.966
you know, the profile of it,

00:05:10.966 --> 00:05:14.860
and then used a procedural tool
in Max called Lathe.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.668
You know, it exists, Revolve,
and also they call it all sorts

00:05:17.668 --> 00:05:20.697
of things in different software,
that basically takes that line,

00:05:20.697 --> 00:05:23.694
revolves it around,
 and you can then

00:05:23.694 --> 00:05:25.993
choose how many segments
you want around it,

00:05:25.993 --> 00:05:29.460
how many segments you want
around the, the spline shape.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:30.970
And you essentially end up
with that shape

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:32.700
in a matter of clicks.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.533
Like, so probably one, two,
 three, four, five,

00:05:35.533 --> 00:05:39.033
maybe eight points
I only needed to make.

00:05:39.033 --> 00:05:41.590
And then the computer
did the rest for me

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:43.320
you know, to be able
to get to there.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.341
We'll be able to take
a look at it in 3D as to,

00:05:45.341 --> 00:05:47.220
how that was made.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.140
This, same thing.
For the body...

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:52.485
Now, my teapot that
I made in 3D,

00:05:52.485 --> 00:05:54.486
if you look at it
from the inside,

00:05:54.486 --> 00:05:56.031
you don't see anything.

00:05:56.031 --> 00:05:57.820
There's no inside to it, per se.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:00.600
It's single-sided.
It has no thickness.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:02.836
I could easily add that, though.

00:06:02.836 --> 00:06:04.936
But the exact same thing
with the body of it.

00:06:04.936 --> 00:06:07.123
It's just a profile line,

00:06:07.123 --> 00:06:09.720
and then lathed
around it, you know,

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:12.025
revolved around, and then
choose how many polygons

00:06:12.025 --> 00:06:14.136
you want and whatnot in it.

00:06:14.136 --> 00:06:17.316
But at that point,
you need the handles.

00:06:17.316 --> 00:06:19.269
You could make them
separate, but if you look at

00:06:19.269 --> 00:06:21.369
a real teapot,
they look like they're actually

00:06:21.369 --> 00:06:23.667
sort of blended
into the surface.

00:06:23.667 --> 00:06:25.531
So what I did then
is I decided to

00:06:25.531 --> 00:06:27.896
collapse it down to polygons.

00:06:27.896 --> 00:06:30.463
So polygons are just,

00:06:30.463 --> 00:06:32.627
you know, a collection of

00:06:32.627 --> 00:06:36.450
vertices and triangles,
and the polys

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:39.015
that we sort
 of discuss is usually

00:06:39.015 --> 00:06:41.596
quad shape, so two triangles.

00:06:41.596 --> 00:06:44.350
But it becomes just
a collection of

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:46.106
triangles at that point.

00:06:46.106 --> 00:06:48.110
And once I was down to that,
if we look at it from

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:49.643
this angle, we can actually see

00:06:49.643 --> 00:06:51.776
there's a line
of symmetry down here.

00:06:51.776 --> 00:06:53.982
So once I had actually revolved
it all around,

00:06:53.982 --> 00:06:56.316
I then collapsed it down
 to polygons

00:06:56.316 --> 00:06:58.809
and deleted half the teapot,

00:06:58.809 --> 00:07:00.879
because I'm gonna
let the computer do

00:07:00.879 --> 00:07:02.672
as much work as
 it can possibly do,

00:07:02.672 --> 00:07:05.436
because I'm lazy is what
it boils down to, right?

00:07:05.436 --> 00:07:08.644
So, you know, once it's
cut in half,

00:07:08.644 --> 00:07:11.549
I can then add another modifier
that procedurally,

00:07:11.549 --> 00:07:13.903
it's called symmetry,
that builds

00:07:13.903 --> 00:07:16.956
the other second half for me and
welds it back together again.

00:07:16.956 --> 00:07:19.009
But that means all I have
 to worry about is building

00:07:19.009 --> 00:07:20.856
half of a handle.

00:07:20.856 --> 00:07:23.516
And so I took polygon spots

00:07:23.516 --> 00:07:26.279
and extruded them out and back
into the other side again,

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:29.911
bridging basically
one polygon around

00:07:29.911 --> 00:07:31.816
and back down
 into the other polygon

00:07:31.816 --> 00:07:34.156
so it became part
of the same shape.

00:07:34.156 --> 00:07:36.013
And then, you know,
had to do some shaping,

00:07:36.013 --> 00:07:38.641
adding loops of, you know,
 edges around,

00:07:38.641 --> 00:07:41.132
and pulling it out
into the shape of the handle.

00:07:41.132 --> 00:07:42.796
Same thing with the spout.

00:07:42.796 --> 00:07:44.753
I took a group of
polygons down here,

00:07:44.753 --> 00:07:46.921
pulled it out
to the end, right?

00:07:46.921 --> 00:07:49.718
And then started
subdividing, you know,

00:07:49.718 --> 00:07:52.060
in between and pulling it
around into shape

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:54.636
and doing a little bit
of shaping with it, you know.

00:07:54.636 --> 00:07:56.216
So it didn't take long to do.

00:07:56.216 --> 00:07:58.694
I built mine with a separate
lid, just in case you ever

00:07:58.694 --> 00:08:01.030
wanted to take it off, you know.

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:02.642
But, you know,
 it could have been literally

00:08:02.642 --> 00:08:05.373
a line from here around
and around and down the outside.

00:08:05.373 --> 00:08:06.876
- That's a wild way to do it.

00:08:06.876 --> 00:08:09.374
If you took half the teapot
and formed it,

00:08:09.374 --> 00:08:11.774
then put a mirror plane right
 here,

00:08:11.774 --> 00:08:13.550
just get a mirror,
then put this thing

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:16.208
up to the mirror,
you would see the whole teapot.

00:08:16.208 --> 00:08:18.600
- Yeah, that's an easy
shape to build

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:21.656
because a lot of it came out
of just procedural tools.

00:08:21.656 --> 00:08:23.716
Other things can be
far more complex.

00:08:23.716 --> 00:08:26.421
You want to create one
 of us, for instance,

00:08:26.421 --> 00:08:29.984
it's a whole different process
to be able to do it.

00:08:29.984 --> 00:08:32.316
- Cool. Well, you've spoken
so much about this teapot.

00:08:32.316 --> 00:08:33.916
I can't wait to see it.

00:08:33.916 --> 00:08:35.937
So maybe we'll just do
a quick change,

00:08:35.937 --> 00:08:38.310
and then we'll get your

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:40.196
model up on the screen.

00:08:40.196 --> 00:08:42.511
- So we're going to leave
Cassidy's Coffee Shop, are we?

00:08:42.511 --> 00:08:44.501
- Sorry, Cassidy.